    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "heading": "Why names",
      "body": "Names are a good place to start reading because they are concrete, familiar, and forgiving. You do not need to know what they mean — you need only to say them aloud. Many of these names you have heard before, in English contexts. Now you will hear them as they sound in French.\n\nThis lesson contains fifty names. Read each one aloud. Check your pronunciation against the audio. Move on when you can say it without hesitation."
    },
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "heading": "The names",
      "body": "Authors:\nMolière, Racine, Corneille, Pascal, Voltaire, Rousseau, Diderot, Montesquieu, Flaubert, Baudelaire, Hugo, Balzac, Stendhal, Maupassant, Zola, Proust, Gide, Camus, Sartre.\n\nPlaces:\nParis, Versailles, Lyon, Marseille, Bordeaux, Toulouse, Strasbourg, Rouen, Avignon, Chartres, Fontainebleau, Chantilly.\n\nHistorical figures:\nLouis XIV, Louis XVI, Marie Antoinette, Napoléon Bonaparte, Charlemagne, Richelieu, Mazarin, Lafayette, Robespierre, Danton, Talleyrand, de Gaulle.\n\nEach appears with audio below."
